Test Operators 

  Bash lests us selectively execute commands when certain conditions are met. 
  We can use test operators to craft a wide variety of conditions, such as whether one value equals another value, whether a file is of a certaintypem or whether one value is greater than another. 
  We often rely such tests to determine whether to continue running a block of code, so being able to construct them is fundamental to bash programming. 

  Bash has multiple kinds of test operators. (Page 28). 
  File test operators allow us to perform tests against files on the filesystem, such as checking whether a file is executable or whether a certain directory exists. 


File Test Operators
Operator Description 
-d       Checks whether the file is a directory
-r       Checks whether the file is readable 
-x       Checks whether the file is executable 
-w       Checks whether the file is writable 
-f       Checks whether the file is a regular file
-s       Checks whether the file is greater than zero

String Comparison Operators 
Operator Description
=        Checks whether the string is equal to another string
==       Synonym of = when used within [[]] constructs
!=       Checks whether a string is not equal to another string
<        Checks whether a string comes before another string (in alphabetical order)
>        Checks whether a string comes after another string (in alphabetical order)
-z       Checks whether a string is null
-n       Checks whether a string is not null

Integer comparison operators allows us to perform checks on integers, such as whether an integer is less than or greater than another. 


Integer Comparison Operatores
Operator Description 
-eq      Checks whether a number is equal to another
-ne      Checks whether a number is not equal to another
-ge      Checks whether a number is greater than or equal to another
-gt      Checks whether a number is greater than another number
-lt      Checks whether a number is less than another number
-le      Checks whether a number is less than or equal to another number

if Conditions 

  In bash, we can use the if condition to execute code only when a certain condition is met. 

if [[ condition ]]; then
  # Do something if the condition is met
else 
  # Do something if the condition is not met
fi

  We start with the if keyword, followed by a test condition between double square brackets ([[]]).
  We then use the ; character to separate the if keyword from the then keyword, which allows us to introduce a block of code that runs only if the condition is met.
  Next, we use the else keyword to introduce a fallback code block that runs if the condition is not met. 
  Note that else is optional, and you may not always need it. 
  Finally, we close the if condition with the fi keyword(which is if reversed). 

Note: In some operating systems, such as those often used in containers, the default shell might not neccessarily be bash. 
To account for those cases, you may want to use single square brackets ([...]) rather than double to enclose your condition. 
The use of single brackets meets Portable Operating System Interface standard and should work on almost any UNIX derivative, including Linux. (Page 29).
  

